TI  - Toxic Effects of Pesticides on Avian Fauna
PY  - 2020
AB  - The best-selling book ‘Silent Spring’ (1962, by Rachel Carson) quivered the international awareness about the role of pesticides for fatally damaging the avian population, but, after more than five decades of such information and advent of various other functionally similar chemicals, still there are overwhelming recent reports of toxicity of pesticides. Globally, over 5 billion pounds of conventional pesticides are used annually for various purposes; these contaminants may act as silent killer of birds. Several studies suggest that the different populations of birds such as songbird, peregrine falcons, ospreys, and Swainson’s hawks are unwitting victims of pesticide contamination.
